course: dictionary
lesson: 7182
title: Personally and whose view it is
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Personally is an adverb, so it tells you how something is said or done. It has a few meanings, and the sentence around it shows which one you need. «Personally, I prefer cats.» Here it means this is my own opinion, not a fact everyone must agree with.
explain: Personally can also mean done by you yourself, not by someone else. «The head teacher personally thanked us.» The head did the thanking herself, so it felt special. Ask: who did it? If the answer is the person themselves, personally fits.
explain: Personally can mean as a person, not about a job or skill. «I don't know him personally» means you have never met him as a friend. The phrase «take it personally» means to feel hurt as if a comment was aimed at you. «Don't take it personally» says the comment was not meant as an attack.
fact: Personally is an adverb; it tells you how something is said or done.
fact: «Personally» can show someone's own opinion, as in «Personally, I prefer cats.»
fact: «Personally» can mean done by you yourself, not by someone else.
fact: «Take it personally» means to feel hurt as if something was aimed at you.
fact: «Don't take it personally» is said when a rule or comment is not meant as an attack.
exercise: «Personally, I think the film was too long.» What does personally show here? | Something done by a robot | *Her own opinion | A job she is paid to do | A fact that everyone must agree with
exercise: «The head teacher personally thanked us.» What does personally show here? | It was a school rule | Someone else did it for her | It was a secret | *The head did it herself
exercise: Sam says, «Don't take it personally, the coach was just tired.» What does Sam mean? | The coach is your best friend | The coach was angry with you | *The coach did not mean to hurt you | The coach is leaving the club
test: «I don't know him personally.» What does this mean? | He is my teacher at school | *I have never met him as a friend | I know him very well and love him | I know his home address and his phone number
test: Ellie says, «Personally, I would pick the blue bike.» Which is true? | Ellie works in a bike shop | Ellie is stating a fact that everyone knows | Ellie has been hurt by the bike | *Ellie is giving her own choice
exam: «A dog guarding its own garden or gate is doing a job; its growling is not about you personally.» What does this tell you? | *The growl is about the dog's job, not aimed at you as a person | The dog wants a treat | The dog has no job at all | The dog is angry with the reader
